"The biggest translation trap in this entire course arrives with a single word: \"spleen.\" In English, the spleen is a fist-sized organ mostly known for filtering blood and supporting the immune system — most people could not name a single symptom of a damaged one. In Chinese medicine, the spleen (脾) is one of the busiest organs in the entire body: it governs digestion, transforms food into usable qi and blood, and keeps blood contained within the vessels. When a Chinese medicine textbook talks about the \"spleen,\" it is describing a functional system that overlaps only partly with what a Western doctor calls by the same English word. This is not an isolated mistake limited to one organ. Every organ name in Chinese medicine — liver, heart, lung, kidney — refers to a broader functional category, not a strict anatomical structure. The \"kidney\" in this system, for example, includes reproductive function and the storage of a person's inherited constitutional strength, roles that Western anatomy assigns to entirely different systems. None of this means Chinese medicine is wrong about anatomy; it means the two systems are answering different questions. Western anatomy asks, \"What does this physical structure do?\" Chinese medicine asks, \"What set of functions travels together, and what should we call that set?\" Once this is understood, a strange-sounding sentence like \"the spleen governs digestion\" stops sounding like bad biology and starts sounding like what it actually is: a claim within its own coherent system, using a borrowed English word that happens to overlap only partially with its meaning. The organs in this unit should be read as functional categories first, anatomical structures a distant second.": "18dbe089ca23",
